\section{Which numbers you get}{

On a fit, \code{summary()} returns the table \code{\link[=fit_mod]{fit_mod()}} recorded at the end of
the optimization — the same numbers as \code{summary(fit)$dsem_coefficients},
which recomputes them from \code{fit$estimated_params} on every call. On the
object \code{\link[=build_dsem_objects]{build_dsem_objects()}} returns, which has no estimates, \code{summary()}
says so and falls through to dsem's method, giving the starting values that
describe the specification you are about to fit.

\code{logLik()}, \code{vcov()}, \code{residuals()}, \code{predict()} and \code{simulate()} are
refused, each naming the Rceattle equivalent: the SEM is one term in a joint
CEATTLE objective and has no likelihood, covariance or simulation of its own,
and dsem's versions would report the unfitted standalone object.
